prompts.trimmer:
Node: {level_name} {path}: "{title}".
Children are: {child_level_guidance}
Candidates: {candidates}
Keep only the best {keep}: the most widely used, correct and distinct ones, covering the whole node with no overlap. Return titles verbatim; do not rephrase, merge or invent.

prompts.critique:
Node: {level_name} {path}: "{title}".
Children are: {child_level_guidance}
Candidate set: {candidates}
Score the set from 0 to 1 against the anchors below, judging: (1) correctness: real products and real operations, no invented syntax; (2) coverage of the node without gaps; (3) no overlap or duplicates; (4) the right level: each candidate is a {child_level_name}, not a parent or grandchild; (5) safety: destructive operations are explicit and evidence is preserved; (6) the title rules: {title_rules}. List concrete issues, and which candidates to remove or add.
Anchors:
{critique_anchors}

prompts.synthesizer:
Node: {level_name} {path}: "{title}".
Ancestors: {ancestors}.
Children are: {child_level_guidance}
Candidate pool: {candidates}
Reviewer notes: {critique_issues}
Why the previous attempt failed: {retry_reasons}
Choose the final children: between {children_min} and {children_max}, never more, and never pad to reach a number. Every title must follow the title rules: {title_rules}. When the children are procedure steps, return them in execution order. Label a child atomic only if it matches this definition: {atomic_definition}

prompts.atomic_verification:
Node: {level_name} {path}: "{title}".
These children were labelled atomic: {labelled}
Definition: {atomic_definition}
For each one, decide whether it truly matches the definition. Be conservative: a child that bundles more than one operation, more than one tool, or a decision is not atomic.
